e if there\'s an error during termination. So, using exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl logs" for those specific pods. But the user needs to specify the pod names. Wait, the kubectl command can be used to get logs for specific pods. So, the next step is to check the logs of the terminating pods to see if there\'s an error. Alternatively, check the metrics for those services. Let me check the available tools again. The get_metrics tool allows querying PromQL. For example, checking for errors in the currency service. But without knowing the exact metrics, it\'s a bit tricky. Alternatively, using get_traces to see if there are any traces that indicate errors or failed requests. But the user might need to first check the logs. So, the next tool call should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the logs command for the terminating pods. Let me confirm the pod names from the previous output. The terminating pods are currency-69f5b55bbc-p6gdk and frontend-proxy-84788b44b-h6g4d. So, the command would be "kubectl logs currency-69f5b55bbc-p6gdk -n astronomy-shop" and similarly for the other pod. But the user can only make one tool call per step. So, maybe start with one of them. Alternatively, check the metrics for those services. But the user might need to check both. However, the tool call can only be one. So, perhaps first check the logs of the terminating pods to see if there\'s an error. That would be the next step.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo investigate potential issues with the terminating pods (`currency-69f5b55bbc-p6gdk` and `frontend-proxy-84788b44b-h6g4d`), I will retrieve their logs. This will help identify if there are errors during termination, crashes, or unexpected behavior causing disruptions in service communication. The logs can reveal root causes like failed health checks, resource exhaustion, or misconfigurations.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage':